The recall search returned these children’s diphenhydramine products:

- **CORRECTDOSE Children’s Allergy Relief** — lot **21011A**, expiration January 2023; Class II, terminated. [S18](https://api.fda.gov/drug/enforcement.json?search=recall_number%3A%22D-1492-2022%22)
- **CVS Health Children’s Allergy Relief Liquid Medication** — lot **80060**, expiration November 2020; Class II, terminated. [S19](https://api.fda.gov/drug/enforcement.json?search=recall_number%3A%22D-1631-2019%22)
- **DrKids Children’s Allergy Relief** — lot **90003**, expiration January 2021; Class II, terminated. [S20](https://api.fda.gov/drug/enforcement.json?search=recall_number%3A%22D-1632-2019%22)
- A **Family Dollar** record listed several diphenhydramine-containing products by SKU, including children’s products, in a recall involving storage-temperature conditions; it did not provide a matching lot number. [S15](https://api.fda.gov/drug/enforcement.json?search=recall_number%3A%22D-1450-2022%22)

The search returned 49 diphenhydramine product-description matches, including adult, institutional, and injectable products, so the results are not limited to children’s products. [S1](https://api.fda.gov/drug/enforcement.json?search=product_description%3A%22diphenhydramine%22&sort=report_date%3Adesc&limit=20)

### What a missing match for `DEMO-LOT-404` means

`DEMO-LOT-404` was not listed in the returned records. That means this query found no exact match in its results; it does **not** prove that a product with that lot is unaffected, safe, or absent from every recall or notice. The MCP lookup specifically identifies its lot assessment as **“not_found_in_text_not_clearance”** and says that candidate matches are not bottle-level clearance. [S1](https://api.fda.gov/drug/enforcement.json?search=product_description%3A%22diphenhydramine%22&sort=report_date%3Adesc&limit=20)

### Details to check

Compare the actual package with the applicable official notice for:

- exact brand and formulation;
- manufacturer, distributor, and repackager;
- NDC, UPC, and package size;
- lot or batch number, including formatting;
- expiration date;
- recall status and relevant dates;
- distribution area and retailer or purchase channel; and
- the complete official recall notice, since the returned enforcement record may not contain consumer instructions.

The search includes historical records, and enforcement records may lag announcements or omit details. The official FDA recall page is [S22](https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-and-availability/drug-recalls).

